    "image": {
      "word": "IMAGE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "An imitation, representation, or similitude of any person, thing, or act, sculptured, drawn, painted, or otherwise made perceptible to the sight; a visible presentation; a copy; a likeness; an effigy; a picture; a semblance. Even like a stony image, cold and numb. Shak. Whose is this image and superscription Matt. xxii. 20. This play is the image of a murder done in Vienna. Shak. And God created man in his own image. Gen. i. 27.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Hence: The likeness of anything to which worship is paid; an idol. Chaucer. Thou shalt not make unto thee any graven image, . . . thou shalt not bow down thyself to them. Ex. xx. 4, 5.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Show; appearance; cast. The face of things a frightful image bears. Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A representation of anything to the mind; a picture drawn by the fancy; a conception; an idea. Can we conceive Image of aught delightful, soft, or great Prior.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Rhet.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A picture, example, or illustration, often taken from sensible objects, and used to illustrate a subject; usually, an extended metaphor. Brande & C.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Opt.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The figure or picture of any object formed at the focus of a lens or mirror, by rays of light from the several points of the object symmetrically refracted or reflected to corresponding points in such focus; this may be received on a screen, a photographic plate, or the retina of the eye, and viewed directly by the eye, or with an eyeglass, as in the telescope and microscope; the likeness of an object formed by reflection; as, to see one's image in a mirror. Electrical image. See under Electrical. distinguished from idolatry; the worship of images themselves. projected in, not merely on, that membrane. of a mirror or lens, which, if it existed, would emit the system of rays which actually exists on the other side of the mirror or lens. Clerk Maxwell.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To represent or form an image of; as, the still lake imaged the shore; the mirror imaged her figure. \"Shrines of imaged saints.\" J. Warton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To represent to the mental vision; to form a likeness of by the fancy or recollection; to imagine. Condemn'd whole years in absence to deplore, And image charms he must behold no more. Pope.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
